**Extra Arguments README**

Some practical CTF examples of what you'd put there:

Performance / compatibility

--force
already added by default, but if you remove it from code this is where you'd put it
--opencl-device-types 1,2 — force CPU+GPU together
--workload-profile 3 — crank up GPU utilization

Brute-force masks (when using Attack Mode 3)

?a?a?a?a?a?a — 6-char brute force, all character classes
?d?d?d?d?d?d?d?d — 8-digit PIN
?u?l?l?l?d?d — uppercase + 4 lowercase + 2 digits

Incremental / smart cracking

--increment --increment-min=4 --increment-max=8 — try all lengths 4–8
--username — if your hash file has user:hash format (strips the username)
--remove — delete hashes from the input file as they're cracked

Output control

--outfile cracked.txt --outfile-format 2 — write results to a separate file
-O — optimized kernel, faster but limits max password length to 32

Basically anything you'd type after the hashcat command in a terminal can go there. It's most useful for one-off flags that don't warrant a dedicated UI control.
